---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Privy vs Attentive for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Compare Privy and Attentive for Shopify launch capture, email and SMS ownership, stock-aware journeys and operating costs.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does store revenue decide between Privy and Attentive?",
    "answer": "No. Use the journeys you need, the staff who will operate them and a quote for the same audience and send plan. A store revenue milestone does not establish a messaging requirement or prove a return."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Attentive replace Privy for list growth?",
    "answer": "Potentially. Attentive offers sign-up units as well as email and SMS. Compare the actual form placement, mobile experience, channel permissions and welcome handoff before removing Privy. Keeping two capture tools is an architectural choice, not a requirement."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare pricing?",
    "answer": "Request the same launch and ordinary-month workload from both suppliers. Include audience size, destinations, message segments, email volume, implementation, support and any commitments. This page does not assert a current minimum or starting price."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I use both platforms together?",
    "answer": "Only when the retained capability is worth the extra coordination. Assign each form and sending journey to one owner, and demonstrate that opt-outs and purchases reach any other active sender before a queued message leaves."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I also consider Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Include it if you are separately evaluating an email platform. Establish its current Shopify event support, consent handling and operational cost before proposing a third system. Adding an email supplier is unnecessary if either shortlisted setup already meets the email requirements."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which has better compliance tooling?",
    "answer": "This comparison does not establish a compliance winner. Have the responsible reviewer inspect the proposed collection experience, supported destinations, suppression behavior and account settings. A platform feature does not itself establish that a particular campaign may be sent."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which handles cart abandonment better?",
    "answer": "Use a purchase-before-send exercise with your actual event path. A platform that queues a reminder but cannot reliably cancel it after a purchase may fail your requirement regardless of its attributed revenue dashboard."
  },
  {
    "question": "What workflow test should decide the choice?",
    "answer": "For a limited-stock launch, test consent collection, product interest, inventory changes and cancellation before release. Record observed behavior separately from promised functionality. Choose the setup that meets those requirements with a manageable operating workload."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Privy vs Attentive</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Attentive for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Compare the complete launch journey: collecting interest, welcoming a subscriber and sending an eligible release alert before stock disappears. Privy combines popups, email and SMS. Attentive also offers collection tools, email and SMS; it does not inherently require Privy for capture or another supplier for email.</p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">This is a requirements-based comparison, not a report of merchant experiments. The proposed limited-stock launch below tests whether consolidation or a split stack makes the release easier to operate. Neither revenue size nor an attributed SMS share determines the answer.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[
          { label: 'Existing Privy store', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Keep a working capture, email and SMS setup if it passes the next launch requirements.' },
          { label: 'Full replacement shortlist', tool: 'Attentive', reason: 'Evaluate its own signup units and email alongside SMS rather than assuming it is an add-on only.' },
          { label: 'Split stack', tool: 'Both', reason: 'Retain a specific useful capability only after verifying audience transfer and cancellation between senders.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Compare launch ownership</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Privy</th><th>Attentive</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Product scope</td><td>Popups, email and SMS</td><td>Sign-up units, email and SMS</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing storefront</td><td>Retain proven placements and journeys if they meet the brief</td><td>Demonstrate replacement placements before retiring existing forms</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Launch audience</td><td>Show how your form records product interest and permission</td><td>Show how sign-up units and Shopify data produce the intended audience</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Queued sends</td><td>Test purchase and stock-change cancellation</td><td>Test purchase and stock-change cancellation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Commercial decision</td><td>Price the proposed capture and sending configuration</td><td>Obtain a written quote for the same operating scope</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Start with the launch you actually need to run</h2>
        <p>A store releasing a small run of jackets needs more than a large subscriber list. A shopper may want one size, already own another item and subscribe only to email. A successful release workflow preserves those distinctions. Sending the whole SMS list an unavailable size is a poor experience even if the campaign receives attributed sales.</p>
        <p>Privy is a reasonable starting point for a team already using its forms and messages: continuity avoids rebuilding a working path. Attentive belongs on the replacement shortlist when its collection experience and coordinated email/SMS journeys meet a requirement the current setup cannot. These are evaluation reasons, not a claim that either product automatically understands variant inventory or supports every condition below.</p>
        <h2>Proposed exercise: early access without a false stock promise</h2>
        <p>Create test records for an email-only subscriber, a subscriber eligible for both channels and a returning customer whose preferred size sells out before release. Use the intended storefront form to collect release interest. Record whether interest refers to a product, variant or merely a general list; do not infer size preference from a page visit.</p>
        <p>Follow each record into the proposed welcome journey. Confirm which system stores the permission and which sends the first message. If email and SMS are both eligible, define their different jobs: email can explain fit and return terms; a text can announce that access is open. A signup must not cause an unrelated old cart journey to restart without an intentional rule.</p>
        <p>Now change inventory while the alert waits. Ask the operator to exclude the sold-out size, pause the release and revise the destination link. Observe whether the change affects queued recipients or only future entries. If automatic stock-aware cancellation is unavailable, document a manual approval checkpoint and who can stop the queue. A broad launch alert may still work if it makes no size-specific availability promise.</p>
        <h2>One capture owner per storefront experience</h2>
        <p>Attentive's Shopify documentation describes a tag that displays sign-up units and captures site events. Its subscriber setup documentation covers email and SMS collection. Privy's Shopify listing describes popups and email/SMS marketing. The decision is therefore which implementation serves the store, rather than a fixed division where Privy must collect every subscriber for Attentive.</p>
        <p>During a staged migration, prevent two overlays from appearing on the same visit. Keep a placement inventory covering the homepage, product pages and mobile entry points. Test dismissal, repeat visits and a shopper who already subscribed through checkout. Preserve the useful current design until its replacement has passed these checks; a vendor switch alone is no reason to redesign every offer.</p>
        <h2>Consolidation versus keeping Privy capture</h2>
        <p>Consolidating collection and sending can reduce audience handoffs and the number of queues an operator checks. It also means rebuilding forms and flows that may already work. Keeping Privy forms while changing the sender can preserve that investment, but only if the connection carries the required identity, interest fields and permission evidence. The presence of a phone number is not proof of an eligible audience.</p>
        <p>For a proposed split setup, ask both suppliers to demonstrate the supported transfer route for your accounts. Measure delay from signup to sender eligibility and from opt-out to suppression. Retry a failed transfer and inspect whether it creates a duplicate welcome. Do not enable both platforms' welcome and cart sends while assuming they will deduplicate each other.</p>
        <h2>Cart recovery during the release</h2>
        <p>A release shopper may join early access, start checkout, then buy before a reminder is due. Test that sequence and inspect each sending queue. Repeat after changing the cart and after withdrawing the relevant permission. Require a clear answer about the purchase event that cancels each journey, including any delay in a split stack. These are proposed acceptance checks; this page has not run them in either product.</p>
        <p>Assess results against the launch's stock constraints. If inventory would sell through without reminders, attributed recovery revenue alone cannot establish incremental value. Where a comparison is feasible, predefine an eligible holdout and compare net contribution, cancellations and opt-outs as well as orders. Avoid introducing a holdout into a promised access entitlement without first deciding how that promise will be honored.</p>
        <h2>Quote a launch month and a quieter month</h2>
        <p>Give both suppliers the same contact population, peak collection traffic, destinations, expected email volume and SMS message segments. Include implementation, support, minimum commitments and any separate sending costs in the written comparison. Ask what changes when the launch audience stays on the list but sending returns to an ordinary month. Do not treat a historical starting price or a store revenue threshold as your budget.</p>
        <p>Hypothetical operating-cost example: rebuilding four forms takes two hours each and documenting two journeys takes three hours each. That is 4 × 2 + 2 × 3 = 14 hours. At an internal planning rate of $50 per hour, the one-time effort is $700 before supplier fees. These are invented planning inputs, not vendor prices or observed implementation times. Replace them with your team's estimates and amortize them over the period you will actually use the setup.</p>
        <h2>When another email platform belongs in the decision</h2>
        <p>If email already has an owner that passes the release and post-purchase requirements, assess the cost of retaining it before migrating. Sequenzy can be included in a separate email shortlist, but this page has not established its current Shopify event path or account entitlements. Do not add a third sender merely to complete a diagram: require a concrete missing workflow and a demonstrated connection before recommending that architecture.</p>
        <h2>Decision record before the next release</h2>
        <p>Retain Privy when the current setup passes the required capture, message and cancellation checks and the replacement adds no valuable capability. Choose an Attentive migration when its demonstrated collection and sending workflow addresses a documented gap and the full quote and migration effort are justified. Keep a split setup only with named ownership of the handoff and a usable failure procedure. Unresolved variant targeting, transfer or account eligibility remains a reason to defer that specific design.</p>
        <h2>Product sources and limits</h2>
        <p>Scope checked against <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">Privy's Shopify listing</a>, <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041854231-Shopify">Attentive's Shopify integration documentation</a> and <a href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408058397588-Set-up-marketing-subscriber-collection-for-Shopify">Attentive's subscriber collection setup</a> on November 24, 2026. These establish product roles, not a performance ranking. Account-specific features, connection paths, destination eligibility and current quotes require confirmation for the proposed configuration.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-attentive">Omnisend vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/privy-vs-postscript">Privy vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-privy">Sequenzy vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
